Chapter VII – Shrine Shadows & Lunar Funeral Hymn
The shrine was quiet, abandoned for centuries. Moonlight filtered through fractured beams, casting jagged lines across the stone floor, where small pools mirrored the night sky. The air was thick with the scent of damp moss and decay, but the stillness carried a pulse of something… unnatural.

Leora stood at the center, black thistle polearm in hand, water swirling around her ankles as if responding to her very presence. She didn’t flinch at the sensation crawling over her skin—the warning tremor of a cursed spirit—but smiled faintly.

“So, this is the little ghost who haunts her own shrine,” a voice purred, echoing unnaturally in the open space.

Sukuna stepped forward, the air around him shimmering subtly, slashes of cursed energy streaking like blades just out of sight. He expected fear, submission—but found none.

“I only haunt places worth my time,” Leora replied, her voice calm, teasing, a flicker of amusement in her eyes. The water rippled around her as if bowing in deference to her will alone.

