Leora tilted her head, black thistle polearm resting lightly on her shoulder. “You should be careful, demon. Some ghosts bite.”

He grinned, sharp and dangerous. “I like biting things that fight back.”

The silence shattered with the arrival of two figures: Amon and Wilhelm, sorcerers intent on testing the girl. Their cursed energy radiated sharply, slashing through the tension with precision and force.

Leora’s eyes narrowed. “So, they’ve sent the meat,” she muttered, toes shifting in the shallow spectral water.

With a deep toll of her black bell, she invoked the domain. The water around her surged, pooling higher, silver-black under the rising Crow Moon. The sky above darkened, a purple haze rolling in, dotted with spectral crows.

“Umbra lunare, regna domus!” she intoned, her voice a blend of chant and whistle. The domain was alive, hungry.

Amon lunged first. Leora spun, polearm sweeping, sending spectral thorns erupting from the floor, entangling him midair. “Spinaregnant!” she shouted. Thorned vines ripped at cursed energy and flesh alike. Wilhelm froze in place as a ghostly crow dive-bombed him, clawing at his aura.

Sukuna watched, eyebrows raised. He expected power, but not this level of mastery. Her control over the spirits, the water, the sound—it was meticulous, precise. And she wasn’t just defending; she was orchestrating a symphony of death.

Little Ghost… he thought, his grin twisting. So defiant. So dangerous.

Amon and Wilhelm were incapacitated, yet Sukuna’s interest had shifted. The thrill wasn’t in defeating her—it was in observing her, in the pull of a spirit unbound, untamed, and mesmerizing.

He stepped fully into the shrine, the floor responding to his malevolent energy, slashes slicing invisibly through the water. Leora turned, eyes locking with his.

“You’re… slower than I expected, demon,” she taunted, letting a spectral crow circle him teasingly.

“Not bad… you play well,” he said, voice low, dangerous, impressed. He swung a hand, sending a slash that the water absorbed, deflecting it harmlessly.

Leora tilted her head, polearm ready. “You’ll have to do better than that, Menka,” she whispered, letting the name slip—a teasing acknowledgment that set his pulse racing.

His eyes flicked up. “Did you just… call me that?”

