Chapter I – The Shrine Where Death Paused
The shrine was broken.

Not ruined by time alone—but abandoned, rejected. The wood had split along its grain, prayer ropes rotted into threads, and the altar had long since lost whatever god it once held.

It was quiet.

Too quiet.

Leora stepped across the threshold anyway.

Her boots scraped softly against old stone, and the sound echoed far too loudly in the hollow space. She didn’t bother brushing the dust from her clothes. Didn’t bother fixing the dried blood on her sleeve.

Didn’t bother hiding the tremor in her hands.

Friedhelm’s face wouldn’t leave her.

The moment—his voice, sharp and urgent.
Run.
The sound of steel.
The silence after.

Her breath caught.

She staggered forward and dropped to her knees in the center of the shrine.

For a moment, she said nothing.

Then—

A quiet, broken whisper.

“…I didn’t even bury you.”

The air shifted.

Not wind.

Not presence.

Something deeper.

Ancient.

Watching.

The First Meeting
“You reek of death.”

The voice came from behind her.

Leora didn’t turn immediately.

Instead, she exhaled slowly, wiping at her face with the back of her hand, smearing dirt across pale skin.

“…And you reek of arrogance,” she replied flatly.

Only then did she look.

He stood just inside the entrance.

Relaxed.

Unbothered.

Like the world itself was something he merely tolerated.

Ryomen Sukuna tilted his head slightly, studying her.

No fear.

That was the first thing he noticed.

No trembling. No instinct to flee. No desperate calculation.

Just exhaustion.

And something colder.

“…You should be on your knees,” he said.

Leora let out a soft, humorless breath.

“I am.”

That made him pause.

Just for a second.

Then—

A grin.

Sharp. Interested.

The Spark
He moved first.

Not a warning.

Not a test.

A slash.

Invisible—instant—fatal.

Leora didn’t dodge.

The ground beneath her moved.

Water—thin, black, shimmering—rose from nothing and twisted, catching the attack just enough to deflect it off course.

The shrine wall behind her split apart instead.

Silence followed.

Sukuna’s eyes sharpened.

“…Oh?”

Leora stood slowly now.

Unsteady.

But upright.

“Was that supposed to impress me?” she asked.

That did it.

He smiled wider.

The Domain Opens
The bell rang.

Low.

Deep.

Wrong.

Leora didn’t raise her voice.

She didn’t need to.

“Umbra lunare…”

The air warped.

Water flooded outward—not natural, not physical, but something between. It poured across the floor, swallowing the shrine whole, rising around their ankles, their knees—

The sky above the broken ceiling darkened.

A moon emerged.

Cold.

Silver.

Unforgiving.

“—regna domus.”

Domain Expansion.

Lunar Funeral Hymn.

The silence shattered.

The Fight
Sukuna laughed.

Actually laughed.

“Good,” he said. “You’re not boring.”

He stepped forward—and the world responded.

Slashes erupted.

Dozens.

Hundreds.

Invisible blades tearing through space itself—

—but the water caught them.

Not fully.

Never fully.

But enough.

Each slash slowed, dragged, distorted—its edge dulled by the unnatural resistance of death-infused water.

Leora moved.

Not fast.

Not clean.

But precise.

“Stay.”

The word hit like a commandment.

The water surged around Sukuna’s legs—tightening, pulling, binding.

He tore through it instantly.

But the delay mattered.

Leora was already in motion.

The black thistle polearm formed in her hand like it had always been there.

She struck.

“Spinae.”

The ground exploded.

Thorns—black, spectral, screaming with cursed energy—erupted upward, twisting, snapping, tearing toward him.

Sukuna vanished.

Reappeared behind her.

A hand at her throat—

She sang.

Not loud.

Not sharp.

But perfectly pitched.

The sound detonated.

A shockwave burst outward, forcing him back—not far, but enough.

Enough to matter.

Recognition
He stopped attacking.

For a moment.

Just watched.

Her breathing was already uneven.

Her stance—tight, efficient, but draining.

Starving.

Exhausted.

And still—

Fighting like this.

“…You’re wasting energy,” he said.

Leora smiled faintly.

“Then stop making me use it.”

He laughed again.

Then came at her harder.

Escalation
The domain deepened.

The water rose higher.

Colder.

Heavier.

“Obediat.”

The bell rang again.

This time, it wasn’t binding.

It was pressure.

The kind that pressed into bone.

Into soul.

Sukuna’s grin faltered.

Not from pain.

From realization.

This could work.

For the first time—

A thought crossed his mind.

She could kill me.

Not easily.

Not cleanly.

But possibly.

And that—

That was rare.

Collapse
She lasted longer than she should have.

Far longer.

But the signs were there.

Her steps slowed.

Her voice cracked.

The water flickered.

Sukuna saw it immediately.

Adjusted.

Pressed.

A final exchange—

A strike.

A counter.

And then—

She swayed.

The domain shattered.

The water vanished.

The shrine returned.

Leora fell.

The Choice
He caught her.

Without thinking.

That annoyed him.

He looked down at her.

Pale.

Breathing shallow.

Still… alive.

Still defiant, even unconscious—her hand barely gripping the polearm that was already dissolving.

Sukuna crouched over her.

A long pause.

“…Pathetic,” he murmured.

But he didn’t let go.

Didn’t crush her throat.

Didn’t end it.

Instead—

He lifted her.

Carried her back into the shrine.

Set her down.

Disappeared.

When She Woke
There was food beside her.

Water.

Clean.

Fresh.

Untouched.

He wasn’t there.

But his presence lingered.

Like a promise.

And It Began
They met again.

And again.

And again.

Fights turned to something else.

Not softer.

Never soft.

But closer.

Pinned wrists.

Breath shared in inches.

Blades that stopped just short.

A game.

Dangerous.

Addictive.

Neither yielding.

Both waiting.

