“You’re the last viable descendant of the Alraune line.”

The air shifted.

Even Sukuna’s eyes sharpened.

Wilhelm spoke now, calm, clinical.

“Your bloodline isn’t symbolic. It’s functional. The Alraune weren’t myth—they were conduits. Half-bound to life, half-rooted in death. That’s why your domain manifests the way it does.”

Leora’s jaw tightened.

“Don’t,” she said quietly.

But Wilhelm didn’t stop.

“You don’t just command cursed energy,” he continued. “You convert it. Refine it. Sustain it. With the right rites, your body could anchor entire systems of sorcery.”

Amon stepped closer again.

“They’re not trying to control you,” he said. “They’re trying to protect you. You’re too important to be alone out here.”

Leora laughed.

It wasn’t kind.

“Important,” she repeated. “Is that what we’re calling it now?”

Her eyes flicked to Wilhelm.

“Or should we call it what it is?”

Silence.

Then—

“A resource.”

Understanding
Above them, Sukuna went still.

Alraune.

The word meant little to most.

But not to him.

He had lived long enough to recognize patterns—bloodlines, techniques, anomalies that bent the rules of cursed energy instead of following them.

And she—

She wasn’t just using her domain.

She was sustaining it differently.

Feeding it.

Growing it.

Like something alive.

“…That’s why,” he murmured.

Why her energy didn’t collapse when it should have.

Why her techniques layered instead of draining.

Why even his slashes had felt… resisted.

Not blocked.

Absorbed.

His grin returned.

Slow.

Sharp.

They want her back because she’s not replaceable.

The Breaking Point
