Leora didn’t pull away immediately.

And that—

That was enough.

Above them, Sukuna’s expression darkened.

Something unfamiliar twisted in his chest.

Not anger.

Not quite.

Something sharper.

Displeasure.

The Fight Begins
Wilhelm moved first.

Not toward Leora.

Toward the ground.

His hand pressed flat against the stone.

“Rise.”

The shrine trembled.

The water came first—Leora’s water—but it twisted, corrupted, responding to him now as well.

Necromancy.

True necromancy.

Leora’s eyes widened slightly.

“Don’t you dare—”

Too late.

The water split.

And something began to form.

Friedhelm
He stepped out of the water like memory given shape.

Not fully solid.

Not fully spirit.

But recognizable.

Leora froze.

“No,” she whispered.

Friedhelm’s head turned toward her.

Empty eyes.

A puppet.

Wilhelm’s voice was quiet behind her.

“You’re strongest when you feel,” he said. “So feel.”

The spirit moved.

Attacked.

Leora Breaks
She didn’t defend.

Not at first.

Couldn’t.

The first strike landed.

Not clean—but enough.

Amon stepped forward immediately.

“Stop this!” he snapped at Wilhelm.

“You wanted her to come back,” Wilhelm replied calmly. “This is how.”

Leora staggered back.

Her breathing hitched—

Then snapped.

The bell rang.

Louder than before.

Violent.

Uncontrolled.

“Umbra lunare—”

The domain didn’t open.

It erupted.

Water slammed outward, flooding everything, rising violently as the sky above shattered into a deep, suffocating dark.

The moon that formed—

Was wrong.

The Alraune Unleashed
“Don’t touch him,” she said.

Her voice wasn’t cracked anymore.

It was cold.

Absolute.

“Don’t you ever use him again.”

The water surged.

Spirits rose in dozens.

No—hundreds.

Not controlled.

Not precise.

Just hungry.

Friedhelm’s echo was torn apart instantly.

Not gently.

Not respectfully.

Erased.

Wilhelm’s eyes widened for the first time.

“…There it is.”

Amon stepped in front of her.

“Leora—listen to me—”

“Stay.”

The command hit.

And this time—

It held.

He froze.

Completely.

Sukuna Decides
Above them, Sukuna was no longer relaxed.

He was watching with full attention now.

Not amused.

Not idle.

Focused.

This is what she becomes.

Not the controlled fighter from before.

Not the exhausted survivor.

This.

Raw.

Violent.

Unrestrained.

And magnificent.

Amon broke free.

Barely.

His own voice surged outward—

“Stop!”

The clash of cursed speech cracked the air.

Leora staggered.

Wilhelm moved again.

More spirits.

More pressure.

They were going to overwhelm her.

Even like this.

Even now.

Sukuna stood.

Decision made.

Intervention
The first slash split the domain.

Clean.

Precise.

Final.

Wilhelm barely had time to react before the attack tore through his defenses, forcing him back hard.

Amon turned.

Too late.

Sukuna landed between them.

Casual.

Unbothered.

Deadly.

“…I was enjoying that,” he said.

His eyes flicked to Leora.

Still standing.

Barely.

Still fighting.

“…but you’re ruining it.”

Wilhelm steadied himself.

“…And you are?”

Sukuna smiled.

That was answer enough.

Claim Begins
The fight didn’t last long after that.

It couldn’t.

Not with him involved.

Wilhelm retreated first—calculating, unwilling to die for a lost outcome.

Amon stayed longer.

Too long.

But even he knew when it was over.

His gaze lingered on Leora.

“…You don’t have to stay with this,” he said.

She didn’t answer.

He left.

Aftermath
The domain collapsed.

This time—

Violently.

Leora didn’t catch herself.

Didn’t try.

She fell.

And Sukuna was already there.

Again.

Catching her.

The Decision
He looked down at her.

At the wreckage.

At the power.

At the potential.

At what she was.

Alraune.

Irreplaceable.

Dangerous.

Desired.

His grip tightened slightly.

“…No,” he said quietly.

Not to her.

To the world.

To the idea of her leaving.

To anyone else taking her.

“You’re not going back.”

He lifted her.

Without hesitation this time.

Not curiosity anymore.

Not interest.

Something else.

Something decided.

