Her eyes hardened slightly.

“Here?” she echoed. “Or with him?”

Amon didn’t answer that.

Instead, he took another step closer.

“I love you.”

The words hung in the air.

Heavy.

Real.

“I always have,” he continued. “Before the tribes, before the expectations—before any of this. You know that.”

Leora didn’t look away.

But she didn’t soften either.

“You’ll be safe,” he said. “You’ll have a life. A real one. Not—” his gaze flicked briefly, sharply, past her “—this.”

Sukuna watched from the shadows of the structure behind her.

Silent.

Listening.

The Truth Revealed
Wilhelm moved forward this time.

Measured.

Deliberate.

“You’re avoiding the real issue,” he said calmly.

Amon’s jaw tightened.

“Not now.”

“Yes. Now.”

Wilhelm’s gaze settled on Leora.

“You deserve to hear it plainly.”

She didn’t respond.

So he continued.

“You are not simply the last of the Alraune line,” he said.
“You are its culmination.”

Sukuna’s attention sharpened.

Wilhelm’s voice remained even.

“Your body doesn’t just carry cursed energy—it refines inheritance. Any child born of you will not only inherit techniques… they will perfect them.”

Silence.

Heavy.

“Guaranteed manifestation,” he added. “Amplified output. Stabilized evolution of ability across generations.”

Amon closed his eyes briefly.

“…Wilhelm.”

But it was too late.

Leora’s expression had gone still.

Too still.

Fortune and Calamity
Wilhelm continued, almost clinically.

“And there’s more.”

Of course there was.

“The Alraune are tied to environmental fortune,” he said. “Where they reside—prosperity follows. Crops flourish. Energy stabilizes. Conflict lessens.”

His gaze darkened slightly.

“And when they leave—”

He didn’t need to finish.

But he did anyway.

“Decay. Collapse. Calamity.”

Leora’s breath left her slowly.

“…So that’s it,” she said quietly.

Wilhelm didn’t deny it.

“The land is already beginning to fail,” he said. “We can’t sustain without you.”

Amon stepped in quickly.

“That’s not why I’m here,” he said sharply.

But Leora was already looking at him.

Really looking.

“…Isn’t it?”

The Proposal
Amon crossed the distance between them.

Close now.

Too close.

“No,” he said firmly. “I’m here because of you.”

His voice softened.

“You think I don’t know what they expect?” he said. “What they want from you?”

A pause.

“I don’t care.”

Leora’s eyes flickered.

Just slightly.

“You won’t be a tool,” he said. “Not with me. I’ll make sure of it.”

His hand lifted.

Hesitated.

Then gently—

He cupped her face.

“You’ll have a life,” he said quietly. “With me.”

Her breath caught.

“…Amon—”

“Marry me.”

The words landed like a strike.

“Come back,” he continued. “Choose me. Not them.”

The Kiss
And then—

He kissed her.

Not forceful.

Not desperate.

Certain.

Steady.

Sincere.

The Shift
Behind them—

Something changed.

Sukuna had been still until now.

Watching.

Listening.

Calculating.

But that—

That was different.

His expression didn’t twist into rage.

Didn’t explode.

It went cold.

Utterly.

Completely.

Disgust curled slowly beneath it.

Not at the act.

At the implication.

He thinks she’ll choose him.

He thinks she belongs there.

He thinks she needs him.

The air shifted.

Dangerously.

The Decision
Leora pulled back first.

Breathing uneven.

Eyes searching Amon’s face—

Looking for something.

Truth.

Doubt.

Anything.

And Sukuna saw it.

That hesitation.

That possibility.

And that—

That was unacceptable.

Intervention
The strike came without warning.

Amon barely blocked it.

The force alone threw him back several meters.

Wilhelm reacted instantly—

Too late to prevent it.

Sukuna stepped forward fully now.

No longer hidden.

No longer passive.

“You talk too much,” he said.

His gaze slid to Amon.

Then to Leora.

Then back.

“…and you assume too much.”

Possession Begins
He didn’t look at Leora when he spoke next.

Didn’t need to.

“This ends now.”

Not the fight.

Not the conflict.

The question.

His presence expanded—pressing, overwhelming, undeniable.

A claim.

Unspoken.

But absolute.

