Buried half beneath earth and time, its stone blackened by something that was never fire.

Sukuna stands at its edge for only a moment.

Then steps inside.

The air changes immediately.

Heavy.

Not with cursed energy—

With memory.

There are symbols carved into the walls.

Not Japanese.

Not entirely European either.

Something between.

Distorted by time.

Sukuna doesn’t slow.

“…Come out,” he says.

Silence.

Then—

something moves.

From the far end of the ruin, a figure emerges slowly from shadow.

Bent.

Thin.

Wrapped in layered cloth that drags across the ground like something decaying but unwilling to fall apart.

Its face—

is wrong.

Too stretched.

Too still.

But its eyes—

are aware.

“…Ryomen Sukuna,” it croaks.

Recognition.

Old.

Good.

“You know what I am,” Sukuna says.

“I know what you were.”

A mistake.

The air tightens instantly.

The creature shudders—

not from fear.

From correction.

“…What you are,” it amends quickly.

Better.

Sukuna steps closer.

“I don’t have time for reverence,” he says flatly. “I need information.”

The creature tilts its head.

“…About the root.”

Not a question.

Sukuna’s eyes narrow slightly.

“You know it.”

A slow, dry sound escapes the creature—something like laughter.

“Everyone who remembers the old systems knows it.”

It shifts closer.

Dragging.

Slow.

“The Alraune is not a woman,” it says.

Sukuna does not react.

But he listens.

“She is a convergence point. Life, death, and inheritance.”

A pause.

“A vessel that does not choose what it carries.”

Sukuna’s expression sharpens.

“That’s incorrect.”

The creature stills.

“Oh?”

“She chooses,” he says. “I’ve seen it.”

Another dry laugh.

“No,” the creature replies.

“…She chooses what she can survive.”

Silence.

Sukuna does not like that answer.

“What grows inside her,” the creature continues, “is not simply yours.”

There it is again.

That implication.

Sukuna steps closer.

“Then what is it?”

The creature’s head tilts further.

“…A continuation.”

“Of what.”

The thing smiles.

Too wide.

Too wrong.

“The first death that birthed her kind.”

The air stills.

Because that—

that is not metaphor.

That is origin.

Sukuna’s voice lowers.

“Explain it properly.”

The creature leans in slightly.

“If she stabilizes—if the bond completes—then what she carries aligns with you.”

A pause.

“If it does not—”

Sukuna’s gaze sharpens.

“Then what.”

The creature’s smile widens.

“It aligns with itself.”

Silence.

Heavy.

Absolute.

Sukuna understands immediately.

If the condition fails—

he does not lose control.

He is removed from it entirely.

And whatever is growing—

becomes something else.

Something not his.

Something not hers.

Something—

independent.

“…How long,” Sukuna asks.

The creature watches him.

“…Not long.”

That is not precise enough.

Sukuna’s cursed energy presses outward—

not violently.

But with intent.

The ruin groans.

Stone cracks.

“Answer properly.”

The creature shudders.

“…Days,” it rasps. “Not weeks.”

That—

is enough.

Sukuna turns.

Already done.

But—

“…Ryomen Sukuna.”

He pauses.

The creature’s voice follows him.

“There is another.”

That earns attention.

“…Speak.”

“The one who watches from the mountains.”

A pause.

“He has been waiting for something like her.”

Sukuna’s gaze darkens.

“Name.”

The creature hesitates.

Then—

“…Shirogane no Yurei.”

A title.

Not a name.

Good enough.

“What does he want.”

The creature’s voice lowers.

“What all of them want.”

A beat.

“To claim what you haven’t secured.”

Silence.

Then—

Sukuna smiles.

Slow.

Violent.

“…Let him try.”

By the time Sukuna returns—

the estate feels different.

Not unsafe.

Not threatened.

But—

aware.

Leora is awake.

Standing this time.

Waiting.

The moment he steps inside—

she turns.

And there’s something in her expression now—

Something sharper.

“You left again.”

Not accusation.

Observation.

Sukuna doesn’t answer immediately.

Instead—

he crosses the room.

Stops in front of her.

Close.

Too close.

“You’re not leaving the estate,” he says.

Her brows draw slightly.

“…That’s not how this works.”

“It is now.”

Blunt.

Final.

Leora exhales slowly.

“You don’t get to decide that.”

Sukuna’s hand lifts.

Grips her chin.

Not gently.

But not to hurt.

To hold.

“I decide everything that concerns what’s mine.”

Silence.

Because that—

that is different.

Not teasing.

Not provoking.

Possessive.

Leora feels it.

“…Something changed,” she says quietly.

Sukuna’s gaze doesn’t waver.

“Yes.”

A pause.

“You’re running out of time.”

Her breath catches.

“…Then fix it.”

“I will.”

“How?”

Sukuna leans closer.

And for the first time—

there is no distance left between them.

“I’m removing every variable that isn’t me.”

Silence.

Heavy.

Dangerous.

And somewhere—

far beyond the estate—

something else hears that.

And begins to move.

