The boy chokes, hands clawing at Sukuna’s wrist.

“I—didn’t—”

“Didn’t what.”

His voice is calm.

Too calm.

“I didn’t touch her—”

“That wasn’t the question.”

Leora steps forward immediately.

“Sukuna.”

He doesn’t look at her.

His gaze remains fixed on the servant.

“You looked,” he says.

The boy’s feet kick uselessly in the air.

“I’m sorry—”

“You don’t look at what belongs to me.”

That word again.

Belongs.

Leora is in front of him now.

Close enough to feel the heat of his cursed energy beginning to rise.

“Sukuna.”

This time—

sharper.

A command.

His eyes flick to her.

Only for a second.

But it’s enough.

Enough for her to step closer.

Enough for her hand to settle over his wrist.

Not pushing.

Just there.

“Put him down.”

Silence.

The servant is barely conscious now.

Sukuna watches her.

Long.

Measuring.

Then—

his grip loosens.

The boy drops.

Collapsing to the ground, gasping, scrambling backward without waiting for permission.

He runs.

Doesn’t stop.

Good.

Sukuna’s gaze follows him only briefly.

Then returns to her.

“…You’re outside.”

Not a question.

Leora exhales slowly.

“I wasn’t aware I needed permission to stand in the sun.”

“You do.”

Blunt.

Immediate.

Her brows draw slightly.

“…This again?”

“This is different.”

