Barefoot.

Silent.

And moves toward it.

She doesn’t make it past the doorway.

Sukuna is already there.

Blocking it.

“…Where are you going.”

His voice is low.

Too low.

Leora stops.

“…You feel that?”

“I feel everything.”

A beat.

“…Then move.”

No hesitation.

“No.”

The word lands like a wall.

Leora’s jaw tightens.

“Sukuna.”

“It’s calling you.”

Not a question.

A statement.

She doesn’t deny it.

“…Yes.”

Silence.

Dangerous.

“Then you’re not going to it.”

She steps forward anyway.

Not aggressive.

Not defiant.

Just—

drawn.

Sukuna doesn’t move.

Doesn’t need to.

Because the moment she gets close—

his hand catches her.

Not her wrist.

Not her arm.

Her throat.

Firm.

Not enough to hurt.

Enough to stop.

Completely.

Leora freezes.

Not from fear.

From recognition.

“…You’d restrain me?” she asks quietly.

Sukuna leans in slightly.

“Without hesitation.”

Silence.

The air outside shifts again.

That presence—

waiting.

Watching.

Testing.

Sukuna’s gaze flicks past her—

into the dark.

“…Come out.”

For a moment—

nothing happens.

Then—

a figure steps into view.

Tall.

Pale.

Hair like silvered ash, falling long and straight down his back.

His presence is—

quiet.

Not overwhelming.

But—

wrong.

Like something that exists slightly out of place.

His eyes settle on Leora.

And soften.

“…So it’s true.”

Sukuna’s grip tightens slightly.

