Chapter L — Containment
She does not sleep alone again.

Not that night.

Not the next.

Not after that.

It is not something Sukuna announces.

Not a rule he states aloud.

It simply… becomes.

A fact.

Leora wakes to him beside her.

Every time.

Sometimes already awake.

Watching.

Sometimes not—but even in rest, his presence is unmistakable. Heavy. Absolute. There is no mistaking that she is not alone.

At first—

she says nothing.

Because she understands why.

But understanding it—

and living inside it—

are not the same.

It begins with proximity.

He does not let her out of reach.

Not in the halls.

Not in the courtyard.

Not even in the quiet spaces she once claimed as her own.

If she sits—

he is beside her.

If she stands—

he is close enough to touch.

If she moves—

he follows.

Not obviously.

Not like a guard.

Like something that has already decided where she belongs.

And that—

is always near him.

“You’re hovering.”

The words come one afternoon, quiet but edged, as she sets a book down with more force than necessary.

Sukuna doesn’t look up.

“I’m present.”

“That’s not the same thing.”

“It is now.”

Leora exhales sharply, pushing to her feet.

“I can’t even breathe without you watching me.”

“Then breathe correctly.”

Her head snaps toward him.

“That doesn’t even make sense.”

“It does if you stop wasting energy arguing.”

Silence.

Tight.

Because this—

this is where it shifts.

Before, their tension had been charged.

Balanced.

A push and pull.

Now—

it tilts.

Because Sukuna is no longer playing.

And Leora—

feels it.

She moves anyway.

Past him.

Toward the doorway.

Not running.

Not defying.

Just—

moving.

And for a moment—

he lets her.

One step.

Two.

Then—

his hand closes around her wrist.

Firm.

Stopping her mid-motion.

Leora stills.

“…Let go.”

Sukuna stands slowly.

Doesn’t release her.

“Where are you going.”

Her jaw tightens.

“Outside.”

“No.”

The word lands flat.

Final.

Her breath sharpens.

“You don’t get to decide that.”

His grip tightens slightly.

“I already did.”

Silence.

And something in her—

finally pushes back.

Not with force.

With feeling.

“…I’m not a thing you can lock in a room,” she says, quieter now—but not weaker. “I chose to stay here. Don’t make that choice meaningless.”

That—

that lands.

Because Sukuna does not misunderstand words.

He studies her.

Really studies her.

The tension in her shoulders.

The frustration beneath her control.

The fear she is not naming.

His grip loosens.

Just slightly.

Not release.

Adjustment.

“You’re not leaving because you want to,” he says.

Her brows draw.

“…What does that mean.”

Sukuna steps closer.

Too close.

His free hand lifts—

resting low against her side.

Right where she had been pressing her hand before.

“You’re being pulled.”

Her breath catches.

Because—

he’s right.

“…That doesn’t mean I can’t decide what I do about it.”

“It does if the decision isn’t yours.”

Silence.

Heavy.

Her fingers curl slightly.

“…Then help me control it,” she says.

Not defiance.

Not challenge.

Something else.

Trust.

And that—

that is dangerous.

Sukuna’s gaze lowers briefly.

Then—

he pulls her closer.

Not gently.

But not harshly either.

Deliberate.

“You don’t control something like this by pretending it isn’t happening,” he says.

His hand shifts—

sliding from her side—

to her back—

spreading wide.

Anchoring her.

“You control it by anchoring yourself to something stronger.”

Her breath falters slightly.

“…And that’s supposed to be you?”

A faint smile touches his mouth.

“It already is.”

Before she can respond—

he moves.

One step.

Turning her.

Pressing her back lightly against the wall behind her.

Not forceful.

But inescapable.

Leora inhales sharply.

“Sukuna—”

His hand lifts—

not to silence—

but to trace.

Her jaw.

Her throat.

Slow.

Measured.

“You’re unstable,” he murmurs. “Your body’s looking for alignment.”

His fingers slide lower.

Resting again where that pull lives.

“And it finds it in me.”

Her breath stutters.

“…That doesn’t mean you get to—”

His other hand comes up—

bracing beside her head.

Cutting off the rest of her words.

“I decide what I get,” he says quietly.

Not cruel.

Certain.

And something in her—

responds.

Not submission.

Recognition.

Her hands lift.

Resting against his chest.

Not pushing.

Not pulling.

Just there.

“…Then stop acting like I’m fragile,” she says.

He pauses.

That—

that is unexpected.

Her gaze holds his.

“I know something’s wrong,” she continues. “I feel it. I feel you. I feel… whatever this is.” Her voice softens slightly. “But I’m still here.”

Silence.

Sukuna studies her again.

Then—

his grip shifts.

Not loosening.

Repositioning.

His hand slides from her back—

to her waist—

pulling her forward instead of holding her in place.

“You’re right,” he says.

A beat.

“You’re still here.”

And then—

he leans in.

Close.

Not quite touching.

“You won’t be if I let you out of my reach.”

Her breath catches again.

Because that—

that is not control.

That is—

fear.

Not spoken.

But present.

For a moment—

neither of them moves.

Then—

Leora exhales slowly.

And instead of pulling away—

she steps closer.

Closing the space herself.

Her forehead brushes lightly against his chest.

Not surrender.

Not defeat.

Choice.

“…Then don’t let me out of it,” she says quietly.

The words settle between them.

Different from anything she’s said before.

Sukuna stills.

Because that—

that is not something he forced.

That is something she gave.

His hand lifts.

Threading into her hair.

Holding.

Not tight.

But firm enough that she cannot step back unless he allows it.

“…Careful,” he murmurs.

But his grip doesn’t loosen.

“You’re making that easier.”

Leora doesn’t pull away.

“…I know.”

Outside—

the world does not stop.

The tribes move.

The watcher waits.

And something inside her—

continues to grow.

But for now—

she stands in his hold.

And he does not let go.

